/*
:name: array_reg_explicit_reset_pin
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: array_reg_explicit_reset_pin
:pyrope_top: array_reg_explicit_reset_pin
*/
// A register ARRAY whose reset is a SOURCE-SPELLED pin, not the module's
// implicit `reset`.
//
// An array's reset is realized as a restore write port (a one-entry-per-cycle
// sweep for a multi-entry array), and tolg minted that port only when the
// module had an implicit `reset` INPUT -- which a declaration naming its own
// reset deliberately never gets (tree_declares_reset_reg skips a reg carrying
// an explicit `reset_pin`). So `= 0` here was silently demoted to a power-on
// `INIT` on the cgen_memory instance, the user write was never gated with
// `!rst`, and the array kept whatever was written DURING reset. This is
// bedrock-rtl br_delay's Pyrope side, which LEC-refuted at the first
// post-reset step (lhdtrack tests/br_delay).
//
// ONE entry on purpose. A multi-entry array restores one entry per clock, so a
// full reset takes N cycles by construction (the documented Pyrope semantics)
// and cannot be equivalent to a golden that clears the whole array on one
// edge -- lgcheck's bounded miter finds exactly that with a single-cycle reset
// pulse. At N=1 the sweep degenerates to a constant address, so the reset is
// one cycle on both sides and the ONLY thing under test is whether the reset
// exists at all. The second half of the fix -- the sweep counter's own clock,
// which came out as `always @(posedge 'hx /*cgen-miss*/)` when the memory named
// its clock too -- is covered because `clock_pin=ref clk` is spelled here and
// any unclocked flop in the emission would refute.
pub mod array_reg_explicit_reset_pin::[timecheck=false](clk:u1, rst:u1, we:u1, d:u8) -> (q:u8@[]) {
  reg mem:[1]u8:[clock_pin=ref clk, reset_pin=ref rst, ordering="old"] = 0
  q = mem[0]
  if we != 0 {
    mem[0] = d
  }
}
